# Allowlist for check-public-artifact.sh.
#
# This repository DOCUMENTS the workstream convention, so its published examples
# are the exact strings the sweep must flag in every other repo. Entries are
# scoped to a file AND to the specific example identifier, never to a whole file:
# a genuine leak appearing in one of these same files -- a private project name,
# an absolute workspace path, a real task ID -- still reports.
#
# The sweep prints the number of lines it suppressed on every run, so growth here
# is visible rather than inferred. Keep it that way: widen an entry only when a
# hit is genuinely published content, and never to quieten a real finding.
#
# Every entry is anchored on the file the hit sits in. The sweep labels a hit
# file:lineno:content in every mode -- in --range mode it reads the file and line
# from the diff headers -- so one entry serves the working-tree sweep and the
# pre-push range sweep alike. Fixture task IDs are pinned by their phase code, so
# a test that mints a new fixture code adds its code here, and the addition is
# read when it lands.

# The state-file format rule, which shows a task pointer, a backlog and a gate.
^\.claude/rules/workstreams-rule\.md:[0-9]+:.*#(DR-[0-9]|G-DR)\b
^\.claude/rules/workstreams-rule\.md:[0-9]+:.*#MG-2\b

# The workstream template ships the Completion phase as literal tasks.
^\.claude/skills/workstream-create/templates/workstream\.md:[0-9]+:.*#CL-[123]\b

# The acceptance test drives a demo workstream end to end, so it names that
# workstream's own tasks and its state path. Both are fixtures, not references.
^tests/va3-acceptance\.sh:[0-9]+:.*#(BD-[0-9]|G-BD|CL-[123])\b
^tests/va3-acceptance\.sh:[0-9]+:.*\.state/workstreams/project/greeting-script\b

# The offline hook fixture builds demo workstreams under a temp directory and
# generates their task lines, so it names fixture IDs, fixture state paths and
# one fixture handoff filename.
^tests/session-start-acceptance\.sh:[0-9]+:.*#(BD-[0-9]+|G-B)
^tests/session-start-acceptance\.sh:[0-9]+:.*\$T/\.state/workstreams/
^tests/session-start-acceptance\.sh:[0-9]+:.*\.state/workstreams/maintain/gamma/workstream\.md
^tests/session-start-acceptance\.sh:[0-9]+:.*\$T/\.state/handoffs/from-elsewhere-20260101-000000\.md

# The record script's test builds its fixture workstreams and ACTIVE.md as
# heredocs under a temp directory, so their task, gate and pointer lines appear
# bare; the IDs are pinned to the fixture phase codes and the paths to the temp
# directory, so a real ID in a real sentence, or a real workstream path, still
# reports.
^tests/record-acceptance\.sh:[0-9]+:.*#(G-(DR|DN|BD|SK|HW)|(DR|DN|BD|PX|SK|HW|MV|DF|ZZ|V|C)-[0-9]+)\b
^tests/record-acceptance\.sh:[0-9]+:.*\$T/(varied/|comp/)?\.state/workstreams/

# The condensation script's test: one long and one short completed record, an
# open task and a gate, and the checks that grep for them.
^tests/condense-acceptance\.sh:[0-9]+:.*#(BD-[123]|G-BD)\b

# This file's own entries quote the identifiers they exempt, so a diff that adds
# or edits one reports every string it names. Exempted by SHAPE: a line of this
# file whose content begins with `^`, the anchor every entry starts with, or
# with `:[0-9]+:`, the anchor of the range-mode entries this file carried until
# the sweep learned to label range hits by file -- retired, but every one of
# them is in published history and in the diff that removed them. Comments and
# prose in this file are NOT exempt, so a private name written into a comment
# here still reports; that was verified by injecting one.
#
# THE HOLE IT LEAVES, stated because a suppression nobody can see is the failure
# this whole file risks: a private task ID written INTO AN ENTRY in this file
# would be hidden, since the entry matches by shape regardless of content.
# Nowhere else -- the same ID in a comment here, or anywhere in the tree,
# reports. Entries are read when added, and the sweep prints its suppression
# count every run, which is the compensating visibility.
^\.public-sweep-allow:[0-9]+:[-+ ]?(\^|:\[0-9\]\+:)
